From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] radv/ps5: RADV_PS5_APU_BUDGET_FIX=1 counts the driver once
 in the memory budget

Driver patch, after lead-mesh-19. Off unless the switch is exactly "1"; read once.
From finding 3 of heydemoura/EglantinePS5 PR 81 (diag/mkw-leak, open; the PR
itself only documents it, this is the fix it names).

radv_query_heap_info on the PS5 reports the driver's bytes as both
allocated_vram and allocated_vram_vis, and both APU paths of
radv_get_memory_budget_properties add the two. So the visible VRAM heap's
heapUsage read twice what the driver holds, and the remaining space of that
heap (vram_vis_heap_size - usage) ran out at half the heap, which cuts the room
VMA's WITHIN_BUDGET allocations get (heapBudget - heapUsage). With the switch
on, allocated_vram is 0 and the driver is counted once, as visible VRAM. That
room then never shrinks; it grows once the driver holds more than about half
the heap. heapBudget itself can read lower, by up to the driver's bytes, since
it is that room plus heapUsage. The pool's free figure and the 2/3 split stay
as they were (the total free space changes only where twice the driver read
more than the pool's used figure). The driver count still includes GTT-domain
buffers, as before; allocated_gtt stays 0.

viejunerd/Eglantine-PS5- docs/MEMORY-CPU-EXPERIMENTS.md notes the same bug
("Texture cache: device memory" and "VMA heap 1: usage" count the driver
twice). Our core's texture and buffer caches already avoid it by default
(performance.h graphics_usage_from_pool: usage from the pool's free block);
this fixes the figure itself for VMA, graphics_usage=driver and the logs.

Not built, not run.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/amd/vulkan/radv_physical_device.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/amd/vulkan/radv_physical_device.c b/src/amd/vulkan/radv_physical_device.c
index c0826e5..bc5d7f8 100644
--- a/src/amd/vulkan/radv_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/amd/vulkan/radv_physical_device.c
@@ -3262,6 +3262,12 @@ struct radeon_winsys_heap_info {
    uint64_t gtt_usage;
 };
 
+#ifdef RADV_PS5
+#include <stdio.h>
+#include <stdlib.h>
+#include "util/u_atomic.h"
+#endif
+
 static void
 radv_query_heap_info(struct radv_physical_device *pdev, struct radeon_winsys_heap_info *heap_info)
 {
@@ -3273,6 +3279,19 @@ radv_query_heap_info(struct radv_physical_device *pdev, struct radeon_winsys_hea
    radv_ps5_winsys_heap_usage(&pool, &available, &driver);
    heap_info->allocated_vram = driver;
    heap_info->allocated_vram_vis = driver;
+   /* EglantinePS5 (heydemoura/EglantinePS5 PR 81, finding 3): the budget's APU paths add
+    * allocated_vram and allocated_vram_vis, and on the PS5 both are the same driver bytes, so the
+    * visible VRAM heap's usage read twice what the driver holds and its free space ran out at half
+    * the heap. With RADV_PS5_APU_BUDGET_FIX=1 the driver is counted once, as visible VRAM. Read once. */
+   static int apu_budget_fix = -1;
+   if (unlikely(apu_budget_fix < 0)) {
+      const char *const value = getenv("RADV_PS5_APU_BUDGET_FIX");
+      const int on = value && value[0] == '1' && value[1] == '\0';
+      if (p_atomic_cmpxchg(&apu_budget_fix, -1, on) == -1 && on)
+         fprintf(stderr, "radv/ps5: the memory budget counts the driver once (RADV_PS5_APU_BUDGET_FIX)\n");
+   }
+   if (apu_budget_fix)
+      heap_info->allocated_vram = 0;
    heap_info->vram_usage = pool - MIN2(available, pool);
    heap_info->vram_vis_usage = heap_info->vram_usage;
 #endif
-- 
